  "content": {
    "id": "p43-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "There are five possible configurations of three distinct planes in space. Two involve parallel planes, and the other three do not. Draw a picture of each one.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "sharedSolutionDiagramLayout": "row",
    "answer": {
      "short": "The five configurations are:\n\n1. Three parallel planes.\n2. Two parallel planes cut by a third plane.\n3. Three planes sharing one common line.\n4. Three planes meeting pairwise in three distinct parallel lines, with no point common to all three.\n5. Three planes with exactly one point common to all three.",
      "worked": "First consider parallel pairs. If every pair is parallel, draw three parallel planes. If exactly one pair is parallel, draw the third plane cutting both.\n\nIf no pair is parallel, every pair meets in a line. The three intersection lines can coincide, giving one common line; they can be distinct and parallel, giving no common point; or they can meet at one point, giving exactly one point common to all three planes. These are the remaining three configurations.",
